## ABSTRACT
Organic-inorganic hybrid perovskite solar cells (PSCs) are attracting tremendous attention for new-generation photovoltaic devices because of their excellent power conversion efficiency and simple fabrication process. One of the various approaches to increase the efficiency of PSCs is to change the material on structure of the carrier transport layer. Here, optically long and electrically short structural concept is proposed to enhance the characteristics of a PSC by employing selectively grown single crystalline \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanorods. The approach has the merit of increasing the electron-hole separation effectively and enables a thicker active layer to be coated without electrical loss by using \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanorods as an electron pathway. Moreover, selectively grown \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanorods increase the optical path of the incident light via scattering effects and enable a smooth coating of the active layer. Nanoimprint lithography and hydrothermal growth were employed to fabricate selectively grown \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanorod substrates. The fabricated solar cell exhibits an efficiency of \(19.86\%\) with a current density, open-circuit voltage, and fill factor of \(23.13\,\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\), \(1.120\,\mathrm{V}\), and \(76.69\%\), respectively. Time-resolved photoluminescence, ultraviolet-visible (UV–Vis) spectroscopy, and the incident photon to current efficiency (IPCE) analysis were conducted to understand the factors responsible for the improvement in characteristics of the fabricated PSCs.
## KEYWORDS
perovskite solar cells, patterned \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanorods, nanoimprint lithography
## 1 Introduction
Recently, as the environmental pollution has worsened, the need for environmentally friendly energy sources has come to the fore. In particular, solar energy harvesting is attracting attention because solar energy is one of the most clean and sustainable energy source [1–3]. Among various solar-to-energy devices, conventional semiconductor solar cells require complicated processes and the processing costs are high, whereas perovskite solar cells (PSCs) are fabricated using simple and low-cost solution processes over large areas [4, 5]. Moreover, organic-inorganic hybrid PSCs have unique properties such as high light absorption and bipolar charge transport [6–10]. Owing to these advantages, they are attracting attention as one of the third-generation solar cells and have been a subject of many studies. A verified power conversion efficiency (PCE) of \(23.3\%\) was reported for organic-inorganic hybrid PSCs in the National Renewable Energy Laboratory's best efficiency chart [11].
Conventionally, PSCs have been constructed with a perovskite layer between an electron transfer material (ETM) layer and a hole transfer material (HTM) layer [12–15]. To fabricate high efficiency PSCs, various studies have been reported to improve the electrical properties by changing a carrier transfer materials. Among various types of ETM layer, mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) or blocking \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) structures have been commonly used for an ETMs, and various morphologies of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) such as nanorods, nanosheets, nanotubes and nanowires have been reported as a concept of ETMs for highly efficient PSCs [16–18]. Especially, one dimensional (1D) \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanorod structures have been studied for PSCs because of the better electron transfer and lower charge recombination [19–21]. However, despite the advantages of excellent electrical properties offered by these 1D nanostructures, the photovoltaic performance of PSCs with cooperating 1D nanorods still lags behind those based on the conventional mesoporous or planar structure because of pore filling and uniform distribution.
