In this study, we report the improvement in the electrical characteristics of highly efficient organic-inorganic PSCs with selectively grown \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanorods (SG-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs) serving as electron pathways. There are many studies on growing \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs on the entire surface; however, to the best of our knowledge, there are no studies on the fabrication and application of SG-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs substrate [22, 23]. Using the substrate with the SG-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs, the light absorption of the active layer was increased by maximizing the optical path and achieving a smooth coating of the active material. This method does not directly improve the properties of the active material but improves the optical and electrical characteristics of the device by means of a selective array of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs.
## 2 Experimental section
To fabricate the SG-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR structure, we used a nanoimprint-lithography-based direct transfer printing technique and hydrothermal synthesis process. First, a template was formed by the direct printing technique to obtain the SG-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs. Conventional photolithography
has a complex process involving several steps such as photoresist coating, developing, and etching to form elaborate nanostructures [24, 25]. Owing to the complex process and high processing costs, it is difficult to apply in real industries. In contrast, direct transfer printing is one of the next-generation patterning techniques with the advantages of large-area and cost-effective processing [26–29]. Moreover, various functional materials can be used as a printing resin in the direct printing method and the process can also be performed on rough surfaces and flexible substrates by employing a flexible mold. Herein, direct transfer printing was used to develop templates for the hydrothermal growth of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) using \(\mathrm{SiO}_{x}\)-based hydrogen silsesquioxane (HSQ), which has excellent thermal and chemical durability as a printing resin [30].
## 3 Result and discussion
Figure 2 shows a scanning electron microscope (SEM) image taken to analyze the structural characteristics of the SG-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR substrate.
Figure 2(a) shows top-view image of the fabricated template consisting of hole arrays with a diameter of \(500\ \mathrm{nm}\) at a period of \(1{,}000\ \mathrm{nm}\). Figure 2(b) shows the selective growth of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs by the hydrothermal synthesis on the opened compact \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) surface and Fig. 2(c) shows the enlarged top-view images of the C-SG-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs successfully synthesized with a reversed-phase pattern of the template. Top-view SEM images of SG-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR and C-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR substrate are shown in Fig. S2 in the ESM. The cross-sectional image (Fig. 2(d)) confirms that \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs with a height of \(450\ \mathrm{nm}\) were successfully grown only in the uncovered regions before removing the template. This result shows that \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs are selectively grown only in certain regions, because the \(\mathrm{C-TiO}_{2}\) layer, which acts as a seed layer, is selectively opened by fabricating a template.
To elucidate the characteristics of the SG-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR, normalized X-ray diffraction (XRD) data is shown in Fig. 3(a). The diffraction peaks could be indexed to rutile \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) with (110), (101), (111), (211), and (220) reflections. Raman spectroscopy (Fig. 3(b)) is a sensitive method to differentiate the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) crystal structure. The Raman spectrum of the SG-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR indicates a rutile crystal with Raman bands at 240.6, 444.3, and \(608.0\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\). In the XRD and Raman spectra data, \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) peak intensities are strong in order of C-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR, SG-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR and C-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) substrate since the amount of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanorods existing on the substrate is large in order of C-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR, SG-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR, C-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images further confirm
the growth of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs, \(400{-}500\ \mathrm{nm}\) in height, on the compact \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer (Fig. 3(c)). Figure S3 in the ESM shows cross-view images of PSCs using \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) , \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) NR, SG- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR substrates. The EDS mapping image of the fabricated device (Fig. 3(d)) indicates that the perovskite is well-coated around the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR. Figure 3(e) shows a high-resolution cross-sectional image and selected area electron diffraction (SAED) patterns for the grown \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR. The X-ray diffraction pattern confirms that the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs are grown as single crystals in the [001] direction.
To assess the applicability of the SG- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs in perovskite solar cells, we fabricated PSCs with the configuration of \(\mathrm{FTO/C-TiO_{2}/}\) SG- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR/perovskite/HTM/Au. Then, its performance was compared with planar-type PSCs \(\mathrm{C-TiO}_{2}\) PSC) without \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs and entirely \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR grown type PSCs \(\mathrm{C-TiO}_{2}\) NR PSC) (Fig. 4). All three types of structures were fully filled by the perovskite to form three-dimensional (3D) heterojunction nanostructures.
Figure 5(a) presents the current density–voltage \((J\!\!-\!V)\) curves for these three types of devices. The \(J{-}V\) characteristics of the devices with \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) PSC, \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) NR PSC, and \({\mathrm{SG-TiO}}_{2}\) NR PSC under \(100\mathrm{\mA/cm}^{2}\) illumination (AM 1.5G) are listed in Table 1. \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) PSC has a PCE of \(16.71\%\) with a short-circuit current density \(\left(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\right)\) of \(21.70\;\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) , open-circuit voltage \(\left(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\right)\) of \(1.025\;\mathrm{V},\) and a fill factor (FF) of \(75.12\%\) . In case of \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) NR PSC, all the photovoltaic parameters including \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) \((22.19\;\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2})\) ), \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) (1.097 V), and FF \((75.19\%)\) , are superior to those of the \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) PSC via using \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs as an electron pathway. This result indicates that the electrical
performance of the device did not deteriorate although the active layer was coated on the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR structure. Moreover, the efficiency of the SG- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR PSC increased further to \(19.86\%\) \((J_{\mathrm{sc}}=23.13\,\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) , \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}=1.120~\mathrm{V},\) and \(\mathrm{FF}=76.69\%\) ), confirming that the SG- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR structure improves the current density. Figure 5(b) shows the external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra for \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) , \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) NR, and SG- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR PSCs when illuminated with monochromatic light over the wavelength range of 300 to \(900\;\mathrm{nm}\) . The SG- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR PSC shows higher EQE than those of the \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) and \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) NR PSCs, in agreement with the differences in the device photocurrents of these two devices. Integration of the EQE spectrum in the range of solar emission yields AM 1.5 photocurrents of 21.29, 22.04 and \(23.35\;\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) for \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) , \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) NR and SG- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR devices, respectively. They have almost the same values as the \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) value obtained from the data in Fig. 2(a) within a \(2\%\) mismatch. The UV–Vis absorption data in Fig. S4 also supports the increase in the IPCE values. In addition, as shown in Fig. S5 in the ESM, the PCE difference between reverse and forward scans was not significantly changed after \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanorod formation. Fabricated PSCs with SG- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR averagely has higher conversion efficiency than PSCs with \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) (Fig. 5(c)), and Fig. 5(d) shows fabricated devices work stably for \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) .
To elucidate the reason why the SG- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR PSC shows higher current density and IPCE value, we analyzed the transmittance and diffused transmittance of the substrates with \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) , \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) NR, and SG- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR (Fig. 6). The current density of the perovskite devices is determined by the amount of light absorbed by the active layer, which is proportional to the amount of light transmitted and the absorption coefficient of the active layer. Herein, the patterned arrangement of the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR between \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) and the active layer can increase the light absorption by increasing the optical path of light owing to the light scattering by the nanostructure. High diffused transmittance changes the path of light passing through the active layer and increases the optical path, allowing higher absorption of light by the active layer. The total transmittance and reflectance of \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) , \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) NR, and SG- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR substrates are very similar (Figs. 6(a) and 6(c)). The transmittance did not decrease even when the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs were grown on the substrate, which can be explained by the effective medium theory and refractive index gradient effect [31, 32]. In case of diffused transmittance, the substrates with the grown \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs clearly have higher transmittances than the \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) substrate. Especially, the diffused transmittance of the substrate with SG- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs is two times higher than that of the \({\mathrm{C-TiO}}_{2}\) NR substrate (Fig. 6(b)). Although the three different types of substrates have similar light transmittances, the SG- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR PSC shows better light absorption owing to high light scattering leading to increased optical path.
NR do not follow a simple mono-exponential decay, implying a nonlinear recombination process. The non-linear decay observed in the TRPL spectrum can be understood in terms of the observed TRPL signal including distinct contributions from different regions with varying carrier lifetimes, which is caused by the expected spatial heterogeneity of the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR. These results demonstrate that \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of the SG- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR PSC can be higher than the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) values of the \(\mathrm{C-TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{C-TiO}_{2}\) NR PSCs owing to the rapid electron extraction and non-linear recombination [34, 35].
In summary, we demonstrated that PSCs fabricated with selectively grown \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR-patterned structures exhibit enhanced efficiencies than those with \(\mathrm{C-TiO}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{C-TiO}_{2}\) NR structures. The proposed approach differs from those previously reported and might offer advantages over those more commonly employed. First, it is possible to achieve a higher \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) value by fabricating PSCs on substrates with selectively grown \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\). It is widely known that the decrease in \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) is caused by the recombination of electrons and holes, and it can be improved by employing \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR as an electron transport path owing to the rapid electron extraction and non-linear recombination. In addition, it aids in increasing the electron-hole separation ability of the active layer by allowing electrons generated far from the ETL to be transferred to the ETL, and enables a thicker active layer to be coated without electrical loss. Another merit of the approach described here is that the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR patterns can increase the light absorption of the active layer by maximizing the optical path of the light. Eventually, a PCE of \(19.86\%\) was achieved with SG- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NR substrate under one-sun illumination.
## 4 Experimental details
## 4.1 Preparation of the substrate
The F-doped \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) (FTO, Asahi glass, 1.8T) substrate was cleaned
for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) in an ultrasonic bath containing a detergent at \(60\,^{\circ}\!C\) and then cleaned with acetone and isopropyl alcohol. A \(40{\-}\mathrm{nm}\) -thick \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) compact layer was deposited on the substrate by spray pyrolysis using a \(20\;\mathrm{mM}\) titanium diisopropoxide bis(acetylacetonate) solution (Aldrich) at \(460\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
## 4.2 Fabrication of the hydrothermal growth template via direct transfer printing
PDMS molds were replicated from a conventional silicon master stamp fabricated by photolithography. A PDMS prepolymer mixture (PDMS precursor and curing agent at a ratio of 10:1) was poured on the master stamp. The PDMS mold was detached after curing at \(90~^{\circ}\!C\) for \(4\,\mathrm{{h}}\). Direct transfer printing was performed using a NANOSIS-280 imprinter. A HSQ (Dow corning, FOX 16 FLOWABLE OXIDE) solution was spin-coated on the replicated PDMS mold at \(3,000~\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(30\,s\). The glass substrate was placed on the HSQ-coated PDMS mold and pressed at 5 bars to transfer the HSQ patterns from the PDMS mold to the glass substrate. The glass substrate was detached from the PDMS after \(5~\mathrm{min}\) and thermally annealed at \(550\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(3\,\mathrm{{h}}\).
## 4.3 Hydrothermal growth of single-crystalline \(\mathbf{TiO}_{2}\) NRs
To synthesize the single-crystalline \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs, \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) compact layer was used as nucleation sites. Hydrothermal growth was conducted in mixture solution of \(30\;\mathrm{mL}\) hydrochloric acid (Sigma Aldrich), \(30\;\mathrm{mL}\) deionized water and \(1\;\mathrm{mL}\) titanium butoxide (Sigma Aldrich). The autoclave was heated at \(130\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) and cooled in air. Then, the samples were washed with ethanol and dried in air. The template was etched off with a buffered oxide etchant over \(10\,\mathrm{{min}}\) and washed with ethanol and deionized water.
## 4.4 Preparation of perovskite materials
Formamidinium acetate and hydriodic acid (57% in water with stabilizer) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. Formamidinium iodide (FAI) was synthesized according to a previously published procedure with few modifications. Briefly, \(15\,\mathrm{g}\) of formamidine acetate was reacted with \(30\;\mathrm{mL}\) of hydriodic acid in a \(500\;\mathrm{mL}\) round-bottomed flask at room temperature for \(1\,\mathrm{{h}}\) with stirring. Then, solvent was evaporated from the light yellow color solution with an evaporator system at \(70~^{\circ}\!C\) for \(1\,\mathrm{{h}}\) to obtain a yellowish power. The resulting powder was washed with diethyl ether until the powder turned white, and then, it was dissolved in ethanol and precipitated thrice using diethyl ether. The recrystallization of FAI was conducted with slow gas diffusion with ethanol and diethyl ether during 3 days. After recrystallization, the resulting powder was collected and dried at \(60\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\). The other materials, lead iodide \((\mathrm{PbI}_{2})\), methylammonium bromide (MABr), and lead bromide \((\mathrm{PbBr}_{2})\) were purchased from TCI, Adlich, and Dyesol, respectively. Spiro-OMeTAD and FK209 were purchased from EM-Index.
## 4.5 Perovskite solar cell fabrication
Prior to perovskite deposition, the substrates with the SG- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NRs were exposed on a UV-Ozone cleaner for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\). The perovskite precursor solutions were prepared by mixing FAI (1.105 M), \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)
(1.21 M), MABr (0.195 M), and \(\mathrm{PbBr}_{2}\) (0.195 M) in anhydrous dimethylformamide (DMF) dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) = 4:1 (v:v). The prepared solution was spin-coated in two steps, first at 2,000 rpm for 15 s and then at 6,000 rpm for 20 s. During the second step, 1 mL of diethyl ether was dropped on the spinning substrate at one time with slowly at the 10 s during the second step. The substrates were then annealed at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\). After that, the perovskite films were cooled for \(10\;\mathrm{min}\) and washed with isopropyl alcohol. A hole transfer layer was coated at 3,000 rpm for 10 s with a solution of spiro-OMeTAD (EM index) in chlorobenzene \((70\,\mathrm{mM})\) mixed with \(38\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of 4-tert-butylpyridine as an additive and \(21~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of a Libis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl) imide (Li-TFSI) solution \((540\;\mathrm{mg/mL}\) in acetonitrile) and \(10\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of tris(2-(1h-pyrazol-1-yl)-4-tert-butylpyridine)-cobalt(III) tirs(bis(trifluoromethylsulfonyl)imide) (FK209, Dyesol). Finally, an Au counter-electrode \(\left(100\;\mathrm{nm}\right)\) was deposited by thermal evaporation under high vacuum.
## 4.6 Characterization and measurements
The \(J_{-}V\) characteristics of the fabricated perovskite solar cells were measured using a solar simulator (McScience, K3000 Lab solar cell \(I_{-}V\) measurement system, Class AAA) at \(100\,\mathrm{\mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\). Before the measurements under illumination, the light intensity was adjusted with a calibrated Si-reference cell certified by nanorod EL. The XRD spectra was carried out with using a D8 Advance (Bruker) diffractometer with an X-ray tube using Cu Kα radiation \((\lambda=0.1542\;\mathrm{nm})\). The morphologies of the samples were characterized by SEM (S-4800, Hitachi) and HRTEM (Talos F200X, FEI). Total transmittance, diffuse transmittance, and absorption spectra were measured with a UV–Vis spectrophotometer (UV-2600, Shimadzu). EQE was determined using a power source (150 W Xenon lamp, 13014, ABET) equipped with a monochromator (MonoRa-500i, Dongwoo Optron Co., Ltd.) and a potentiostat (IviumStat, IVIUM). Steady-state PL and TRPL measurements were carried out using a Chronos BH Fluorescence lifetime spectrometer (ISS, USA) with 100 ps time resolution and deconvolution analysis. Pulsed excitation sources emitting at 515 and \(690\;\mathrm{nm}\) were employed for time-resolved PL measurements.
